Atomics over the compiler's `__atomic` builtins: `ca::atomic<T>` for integers and pointers,
plus thread and signal fences. Only lock-free types are accepted, so nothing links
libatomic and every operation is async-signal-safe: a signal handler may touch the same
atomics as the thread it interrupted.

The memory order is a template argument, not a run-time one, so a wrong order is a compile
error and the call costs exactly the instruction the order needs. Every operation defaults to
`seq_cst`; spell the weaker order where it is enough.

```c++
#include "ca/atomic.hpp"

auto main(void) -> int {
	ca::atomic<int> hits{0};
	hits.fetch_add<ca::memory_order::relaxed>(3);
	hits.fetch_sub(1);
	if (hits.load<ca::memory_order::acquire>() != 2)
		return 1;

	int expected = 2;
	if (!hits.compare_exchange_strong(expected, 10) || hits.load() != 10)
		return 2;
	expected = 2;
	if (hits.compare_exchange_strong(expected, 20) || expected != 10)
		return 3;
	return 0;
}
```

A failed compare-exchange writes the current value into `expected`, so a retry loop needs no
extra load. `compare_exchange_weak` may fail spuriously and belongs in such loops; its failure
order defaults from the success order (`acq_rel` fails as `acquire`, `release` as `relaxed`).

```c++
#include "ca/atomic.hpp"

static auto store_max(ca::atomic<unsigned>& slot, const unsigned value) noexcept -> void {
	unsigned seen = slot.load<ca::memory_order::relaxed>();
	while (seen < value && !slot.compare_exchange_weak<ca::memory_order::release>(seen, value)) {
	}
}

auto main(void) -> int {
	ca::atomic<unsigned> peak{};
	store_max(peak, 7U);
	store_max(peak, 3U);
	return peak.load() == 7U ? 0 : 1;
}
```

The fetch operations (`fetch_add`, `fetch_sub`, `fetch_and`, `fetch_or`, `fetch_xor`) exist
for integers other than bool and return the previous value; pointers get `load`, `store`,
`exchange` and the compare-exchanges. Atomics are neither copyable nor assignable.

`ca::atomic_thread_fence<M>()` orders memory between threads without an atomic object;
`ca::atomic_signal_fence<M>()` only stops the compiler from reordering across it, which is
all a thread needs against its own signal handlers.
